hyperactivity-is-the-theory-of-knowledge-graphs-lines-63 = cons "There is no canonical state and no privileged server owning the graph (§10.6, §10.8). Under content identity (`ContentAddressed chk`), store union is commutative (`ca-union-comm`), and `peer-order-invariant` proves `eval-peer s1 s2 chk p ≡ eval-peer s2 s1 chk p` for all valid peer stores `s1`, `s2` and every computation `p`: there is no client/server asymmetry at the semantic level. Conversely, an adversarial peer cannot corrupt a computation: `peer-forged-rejected` and `peer-forged-eval` prove that any remote object failing local verification (`chk k v ≡ false`) is rejected at the port boundary as absent (`inl unit`). Each participant holds its own local view over shared content-addressed objects; web browsers, terminals, and file trees are merely boundary projections for legacy environments, not the ontology of the graph." (cons "" nil)
